## Why This Is Asked\nReasoning: probes practical memory reclamation and non-blocking synchronization in kernel-like data structures.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- Read-Copy-Update (RCU) semantics for updates\n- Grace period, synchronize_rcu, safe reclamation\n- Memory ordering on x86_64: release stores, READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE\n- Per-bucket isolation to minimize cross-core traffic\n\n## Code Example\n```c
// Sketch
struct bucket { int value; struct bucket *next; };
void reader(struct bucket *head) {
  rcu_read_lock();
  struct bucket *b = READ_ONCE(head);
  int v = READ_ONCE(b->value);
  rcu_read_unlock();
}
void writer(struct bucket **head) {
  struct bucket *nb = malloc(...);
  nb->value = newval;
  nb->next = READ_ONCE(*head);
  if (cmpxchg(head, nb->next, nb)) {
    synchronize_rcu();
  }
}
```
